src/backend/access/prolly/README

Prolly storage
==============

This directory holds version control for Postgres: tables that can be
branched, committed, diffed, merged, cloned, pushed and pulled.

A database's versioned schemas (CREATE SCHEMA ... VERSIONED) are the part
under version control.  Their tables use the table access method "prolly"
(prolly_table.c): heap's pages, read and written by heap's code, kept in
block-mapped storage (storage/smgr/bmap.c), so each branch's copy of a
table shares its pages copy-on-write with the others.  That working set is
ordinary Postgres: MVCC, WAL, indexes, constraints and triggers all work
as they do on heap.

A commit folds the session branch's versioned schemas into canonical,
content-addressed prolly trees, kept in the database's chunk store, and
names the canonical root (canon_folded.c).  Diffs and merges compare
those trees; remotes exchange their chunks.


Interfaces and implementations
------------------------------

Every component is an interface with implementations behind it, so any
design choice can be changed later by writing a new implementation:

	Interface         Header                  Implementations
	chunker           prolly_chunker.h        bytes, stub
	content_hash      prolly_hash.h           sha256, stub
	key_encoder       prolly_keyenc.h         ordered, stub
	value_encoder     prolly_valenc.h         canonical, stub
	reclamation       prolly_reclaim.h        copying, stub
	refs              prolly_refs.h           copies, stub
	transfer          prolly_transfer.h       chunks, stub
	diff_merge        prolly_merge.h          folded, stub
	tree              prolly_tree.h           cow, stub
	table_keying      prolly_keying.h         pk_or_prefix, stub
	canonicalizer     prolly_canon.h          folded, stub
	chunk_store       prolly_chunkstore.h     journal
	chunk_format      prolly_chunkformat.h    basic
	row_applier       prolly_applier.h        replication, stub
	table_file        prolly_tablefile.h      basic, stub
	change_finder     prolly_changes.h        pages, stub
	remote            prolly_remote.h         file
	value_memo        prolly_memo.h           shared, stub
	writers           prolly_writers.h        modes, stub

An interface header declares a routine struct: a ProllyImplInfo (interface,
name, impl_id, format_version) followed only by callback pointers, with the
contract written as comments.  An implementation is one or more source files
here, named <interface>_<implementation>.c, that define a const routine
struct.  Nothing else includes an implementation's private headers or calls
its functions.

Callers go through PROLLY_CALL(routine, callback, args...) (prolly_impl.h).
A NULL callback means "not implemented", and PROLLY_CALL raises
ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED naming the interface, implementation and
callback.  So a stub is a routine with every callback NULL, and a real
implementation can be filled in one callback at a time.

Built-in implementations are listed in src/include/access/prolly_impllist.h,
the one place that names them, and found by interface and name through the
registry (prolly_registry.c).  Libraries in shared_preload_libraries can
register more with prolly_register_impl().  Interfaces keep their numbers,
so the enum has gaps where interfaces were removed;
prolly_interface_valid() says which numbers are interfaces.

Which implementation is used comes from configuration (prolly_config.c),
never from code: a prolly table's history is folded with its access
method's profile (keying, encoders, hash, chunker, trees), and a
database's version control is its profile (refs, transfer, diff and
merge, canonicalizer).


Canonical values
----------------

Rows and keys are folded in canonical form: the same bytes on every
platform, in every cluster and session, whatever the table's DDL history,
with no local OIDs.  prolly_datum.c holds the canonical encoding of single
values, shared by the key and value encoders as a fixed part of the format:
send formats produced with the client encoding fixed to UTF-8
(SetClientEncodingOverride in mbutils.c), and OID-free encodings of arrays
and composites.  valenc_canonical.c stores live columns only, without
trailing NULLs, and decodes rows encoded under an earlier layout.


Trees and chunks
----------------

A tree is an immutable sorted map identified by its root node
(prolly_tree.h).  tree_cow.c builds trees bottom up, cutting each level into
nodes with the chunker; the root is the first level with one node, so the
shape and every hash depend on the items alone.  apply() takes a sorted
batch of edits and re-chunks a window around each one, a level at a time,
until the new boundaries meet an old one; everything after that point is
shared with the old tree.  Nodes are chunks (chunkformat_basic.c) in the
database's chunk store (chunkstore_journal.c): an append-only journal,
later conjoined into table files (tablefile_basic.c), with the store's root
moved by commit records and its appends logged by the ProllyChunks WAL
resource manager (prolly_xlog.h).

Large values stay TOAST's in the working set.  A commit stores a column
whose canonical bytes are longer than 2048 as a reference to a value tree
of its own (valenc_canonical.c, through the tree's value_write()), so
rows holding the same value share it, and a row that is still long goes
out of line whole.  Value trees are cut by a rolling hash and addressed
by byte counts, so an insertion rewrites the chunks around it and a
slice reads only its own.  A leaf records the references its value
holds, so walks reach those trees without knowing the rows' layout.  The
value memo (memo_shared.c) maps a TOAST pointer a fold met to the
reference it made, so the next fold that meets the pointer (an UPDATE of
another column kept it) doesn't read the value again.


Writers
-------

Every write to a branch is noted before it is made (branch_note_write(),
catalog/branch.h): rows of prolly tables, versioned sequences, and
versioned objects' catalog rows, unless made on the session's behalf by
a version-control call.  The writers ("modes", writers_modes.c) decide
whether the write may go ahead.  In the shared mode every session writes
every branch, as in Dolt.  In the owned mode a dirty branch is one
writer's (dolt.writer, by default the session): its pg_branch row records
the owner, claimed by the first write and forgotten once a commit or
reset leaves the branch clean, and anyone else's write is refused.  The
mode is a database setting kept in the refs.


Commits
-------

Table keying (keying_pkprefix.c) keys a folded table by its primary key,
or, without one, by its rows' leading bytes and content hash with a count
of identical rows.  A commit folds each table's rows into its data tree:
in full the first time, then from its baseline, re-reading only the rows on
pages changed since (change finder "pages", changes_pages.c).  The refs
("copies", refs_copies.c) keep branch heads, baselines, tags, remotes and
merge state in the chunk store's root.  Diff and merge ("folded",
diffmerge_folded.c) compare canonical trees table by table, reading only
subtrees that differ; moving a branch to another commit writes the
differences back through the row applier (applier_replication.c), as
logical replication's apply worker writes rows.


Remotes
-------

Transfer ("chunks", transfer_chunks.c) moves the chunks a commit reaches
that the other side lacks, re-hashing each chunk it receives.  A remote
("file", remote_file.c) is a directory of table files and a manifest.


SQL
---

prolly_dolt.c is the dolt_* functions and system views, with Doltgres's
arguments and results, so scripts written for Doltgres run here.
prolly_vcs.c adds prolly_root_hash() and prolly_checkpoint().


Core hooks
----------

prolly_shmem.c is a shared-memory subsystem (subsystemlist.h) reserving
an area per chunk store and value memo implementation that asks for one, and
CheckPointProlly() runs in CheckPointGuts().  createdb() calls
ProllyCopyDatabase() (prolly_database.c) to copy the template's chunk
store.  A temporary or unlogged table is never stored by prolly
(prolly_table_am_for_persistence()).


Tests
-----

src/test/modules/test_prolly runs each interface's conformance suite
against every registered implementation, and tests version control from
SQL and TAP:

	make -C src/test/modules/test_prolly check

src/test/prolly runs Postgres's own regression suites, sqllogictest and
Dolt's scenarios against prolly tables.
